<title>DENMARK: Danish Sept North Sea oil production rises 2.3 pct.</title>
<headline>Danish Sept North Sea oil production rises 2.3 pct.</headline>
<dateline>COPENHAGEN 1996-10-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Danish Underground Consortium (DUC) oil production from its North Sea fields rose 2.3 percent in September from August, operator A.P. Moeller Group said on Monday.</p>
<p>A spokesman said oil production was 900,000 tonnes in September or 219,754 barrels per day (bpd), compared with 214,845 bpd in August.</p>
<p>DUC is responsible for virtually the entire output from Denmark's North Sea oil and gas fields. Denmark is the third biggest producer in western Europe after Norway and Britain.</p>
<p>Production data (in thousand bpd):</p>
<p> Field		     Sept 96	  Aug 96	 Sept 95</p>
<p> Dan			   67.3	    69.7	    65.3</p>
<p> Kraka			  5.3	     4.9	     7.7</p>
<p> Regnar			 0.9	     0.8	     1.3</p>
<p> Gorm			  52.9	    52.0	    44.0</p>
<p> Skjold			30.9	    30.6	    37.8</p>
<p> Rolf			   4.4	     4.1	     4.3</p>
<p> Dagmar			 0.4	     0.4	     0.5</p>
<p> Tyra			  20.3	    19.7	     25.1</p>
<p> Valdemar		     2.6	     3.0	     3.2</p>
<p> Roar			   7.8	     5.8		-</p>
<p> Svend			 26.9	    23.8		- Total</p>
<p> TOTAL			219.8	   214.8	   189.3</p>
<p>A.P. Moeller Group owns 39 percent of DUC. Its two partners, Royal Dutch/Shell group and Texaco Inc, own 46 percent and 15 percent respectively. --Chris Follett, Copenhagen newsroom, +45 33 96950</p>
